WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.730
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:08.480
THOMAS MARTIN: So my
name's Thomas Martin.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:11.300
I'm the founder and
CEO of BigCo, SmallCo.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:13.130
And so we are a
technology integrator

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:16.580
that brings startup technology
and innovative technologies

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:18.680
to the big company enterprise.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:21.950
Previously, I was a CIO
and CTO across multiple GE

00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:23.900
industrial businesses
and had the pleasure

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.540
of leading the effort
to migrate over 9,000

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:28.310
workloads to public cloud.

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:30.117
And today, I'm here
with Brian Johnson.

00:00:30.117 --> 00:00:31.950
BRIAN JOHNSON: Hi, my
name is Brian Johnson.

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:34.250
I'm the CEO and
co-founder at DivvyCloud.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:37.825
Prior to taking on this journey,
I worked at Electronic Arts

00:00:37.825 --> 00:00:39.200
for about seven
and a half years,

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:41.827
working on massive
multiplayer online games,

00:00:41.827 --> 00:00:43.910
deploying a lot of those
games around data centers

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:46.430
around the world and led
the charge into migration

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:48.020
to cloud in about 2013.

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:50.000
THOMAS MARTIN: Very cool.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:50.500
All right.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:53.297
BRIAN JOHNSON: So through
that process of the migration,

00:00:53.297 --> 00:00:55.630
one of the things that we
thought was really interesting

00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:58.720
is that as we adopted cloud,
we started treating it

00:00:58.720 --> 00:00:59.933
like a technology problem.

00:00:59.933 --> 00:01:01.600
But at the end of the
day, that actually

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:02.825
wasn't the case, right?

00:01:02.825 --> 00:01:05.200
At the end of the day, it was
actually a culture problem.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:07.600
And what we realized is
that shift to self-service

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.240
was incredibly important
for our ability to compete.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.370
All of our competitors were out
there building games, and doing

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:14.578
it much faster, and getting
those products to market,

00:01:14.578 --> 00:01:16.330
and learning from
their customers,

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:18.100
and building really cool games.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.683
How could we
continue to do that?

00:01:19.683 --> 00:01:21.100
How could we
continue to innovate?

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:23.715
Well, we couldn't do that if
IT continued to get in the way.

00:01:23.715 --> 00:01:25.840
So we needed to find a way
to allow our engineering

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:27.855
organizations to
access cloud, to be

00:01:27.855 --> 00:01:30.520
able to deploy applications,
to innovate through their cloud

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:32.890
infrastructure without
security and IT

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:34.540
really getting in
the way of that.

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:36.220
And through that
process, what we

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:39.850
discovered is it wasn't just
a technology problem, right?

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:41.943
It was actually three
things that came together

00:01:41.943 --> 00:01:43.360
in this sort of
perfect storm that

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:45.430
caused an issue for security.

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:47.410
Number one, [INAUDIBLE]
we were moving--

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:48.395
we had more, right?

00:01:48.395 --> 00:01:49.270
Just more everything.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:50.140
We had more servers.

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:52.150
So we were moving
from 500 servers,

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:53.860
although that was not
the actual number.

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:55.620
Thousands of servers
to tens and 20,000's

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:58.900
of instances inside
of AWS and GCP.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:01.102
So that was really interesting
through that process.

00:02:01.102 --> 00:02:02.560
And then we also
had this mechanism

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.727
where a number of people
touching the infrastructure

00:02:04.727 --> 00:02:05.770
dramatically increased.

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:08.110
It wasn't just the
IT staff anymore.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:10.900
It was thousands of
engineers all over the globe,

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:13.090
touching infrastructure
and deploying applications,

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:14.570
which led to the third thing.

00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:16.540
We were no longer
server huggers, right?

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:18.740
We were basically
having change everyday.

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:21.520
CI/CD process, one little
code change, a bug fix,

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:23.590
tear everything down,
rebuild it back again.

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:25.337
So that led to just
a dramatic increase

00:02:25.337 --> 00:02:27.920
in number of changes that were
occurring in the infrastructure

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:28.880
any given moment.

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:30.670
So these three things combined--

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:32.380
number of resources
you had to manage,

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:34.210
number of people
touching those resources,

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:36.310
and how often those
resources were changing

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:38.780
led to this incredibly
difficult problem.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:40.960
How do we deal with this scale?

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:43.295
How could we possibly,
as an organization,

00:02:43.295 --> 00:02:44.920
understand everything
that was changing

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:47.260
and react to it in a
reasonable amount of time,

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:48.880
based on traditional
IT and security

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:52.040
processes we had in place,
which were super slow?

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:54.580
So that was really the
problem we see out there.

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:56.430
This is not a technology issue.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:58.802
This is a company
transformation issue.

00:02:58.802 --> 00:03:00.010
How do you get ahead of this?

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:01.302
And how do you deal with scale?

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:05.370
THOMAS MARTIN: So with that,
let's just kind of talk about--

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:06.920
I mean, what does this
really begin to mean

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:08.450
from a practical aspect, right?

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:10.310
I mean, fairly simple, right?

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:13.760
This is just a simple
three tier architecture.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.543
The opportunities actually
seem quite small, right?

00:03:16.543 --> 00:03:17.960
I mean, you've
just got a couple--

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:19.460
you've got a load
balancer, a couple

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:23.600
computes spanning across a
couple of availability zones.

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:26.690
We've got a cloud
storage and Cloud SQL.

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:30.500
Point is, is if you're just
managing it in a small scale

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:33.450
with a single team, not
all that hard, right?

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:35.180
But really, when you
start looking at it,

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:37.910
there's at least 20
plus opportunities

00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:40.310
for missed configuration,
just in this simple three tier

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:41.580
architecture.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:43.670
Think about that when you
begin to migrate, say,

00:03:43.670 --> 00:03:47.600
five, seven, 10,000 applications
across an enterprise.

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:50.180
To try to manage this
at any kind of scale

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:52.100
just becomes unwielding.

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.235
And what I found was
actually, for ourselves,

00:03:54.235 --> 00:03:55.610
in my past
experience, has really

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:58.940
been somewhere between about
100 and 200 applications.

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:01.820
The team, just the whole
structure starts to fall down.

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:05.030
You really have to begin
to think about not only is

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:07.550
that CI/CD process so
important, but it's really

00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:09.830
about all the
configurations, not only

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:12.935
real time upon deployment,
but ongoing and on forward.

00:04:17.149 --> 00:04:19.000
BRIAN JOHNSON: So what
does this lead to?

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:21.570
Well, in our case, it led to
a couple of different things.

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:22.960
It led to loss of control.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:23.570
Right?

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:26.045
We're letting engineers go and
sort of deploy [INAUDIBLE]..

00:04:26.045 --> 00:04:26.920
That's a great thing.

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:27.420
Right?

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:28.518
You want that innovation.

00:04:28.518 --> 00:04:29.560
You need that innovation.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:30.935
In order to survive
as a company,

00:04:30.935 --> 00:04:33.647
you've got to find a way to
compete through innovation.

00:04:33.647 --> 00:04:35.980
So it was really important,
but you lose control, right?

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:37.270
It used to be that
any change that's

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:38.410
going to be made in
the infrastructure

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:39.640
went through us first.

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:41.350
So we wouldn't know a problem.

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:42.340
We would see a mistake.

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:43.900
We'd be able to stop
[? it from happening. ?]

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:45.760
That's not necessarily
the case anymore.

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:47.640
And furthermore,
what's interesting is

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:50.540
this happened really quickly,
all things considered.

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:53.290
So it means that you went from
having an IT organization who

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:55.828
had [INAUDIBLE]
process in place to be

00:04:55.828 --> 00:04:58.120
able to catch these issues
through the sort of controls

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:00.330
and gateways that
had to engineers

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:02.440
just doing all sorts of
things all over the place.

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:04.732
And the problem is nobody
ever sat down the engineering

00:05:04.732 --> 00:05:07.180
organization and explained
to them 20 years of history,

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:09.580
the security issues that we hit.

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:12.730
It's not like IT learned
that stuff the easy way.

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:13.630
We got compromised.

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:14.427
We had problems.

00:05:14.427 --> 00:05:15.010
We had issues.

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:16.750
And so we learned
to build processes.

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:19.245
Unfortunately, those processes
really slowed us down.

00:05:19.245 --> 00:05:20.620
And the other
thing we recognized

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:22.090
is when we started to
move towards a more

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:24.090
cloud-native approach,
we were like, well, we'll

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:25.060
just do alerting.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:26.920
We'll just basically
get alerts every time

00:05:26.920 --> 00:05:28.837
there's something we
need to pay attention to.

00:05:28.837 --> 00:05:30.530
That really quickly
got out of control.

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:31.480
I mean, it just
became whack-a-mole.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:33.188
There was just no way
to keep up with it.

00:05:33.188 --> 00:05:35.500
I have alert fatigue on
here is what we talk about.

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:37.330
And that is getting those
Slack message or emails.

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:39.320
And how do you know which
one to pay attention to?

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:41.590
Where are the important
areas, because the reality is

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:43.600
of 20,000 changes now
you're dealing with,

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:46.000
there's going to be one of those
that might be really important.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:47.890
And you may have a hard time
identifying which one of those

00:05:47.890 --> 00:05:49.580
you need to pay attention to.

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:53.740
So really, this is really about
a signal and noise problem.

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:55.240
With all these
things going on, how

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:58.940
do you reduce all of the noise
so you can focus on the signal?

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:01.570
At the end of the day, you'll
have to leverage automation

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:02.380
to do that.

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:05.710
There's just no way that we can
leverage traditional IT process

00:06:05.710 --> 00:06:08.110
to use a runbook,
to correct problems,

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:10.693
to contact the person to talk
to them about making the change.

00:06:10.693 --> 00:06:12.527
By the time that's
occurred, the application

00:06:12.527 --> 00:06:14.550
has been torn down and
redeployed three times.

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:14.870
THOMAS MARTIN: Yeah.

00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:15.745
BRIAN JOHNSON: Right?

00:06:15.745 --> 00:06:17.830
So you need to be able
to get rid of the noise,

00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:20.350
leveraging automation,
so that your IT staff,

00:06:20.350 --> 00:06:23.260
so your security staff, so
your sec ops, your cloud ops

00:06:23.260 --> 00:06:25.780
have the ability to
focus on that 10%

00:06:25.780 --> 00:06:27.630
that they need to be
dealing with on a more

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:28.720
manual and active basis.

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:33.520
THOMAS MARTIN: And so with that,
you really start to look at

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:38.050
and say that traditional
IT perimeter and processes

00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:42.490
that we've always used and
relied upon are ineffective.

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:45.960
You just can't handle those
kinds of changes at scale.

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:46.960
They're still important.

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:49.370
I'm not mitigating the
fact of perimeter control.

00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:51.078
And we're going to
talk a little bit more

00:06:51.078 --> 00:06:53.590
about that, about how
that begins to fold in.

00:06:53.590 --> 00:06:56.070
But it's really so
important, particularly

00:06:56.070 --> 00:06:59.190
as Brian talked about to be
able to filter out the noise.

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:02.110
And so as you step back
and think about it,

00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:05.980
for those of you who are working
those large enterprise firms,

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:09.640
think about, at least for me,
the IT procurement process,

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:13.150
our development teams literally
had-- they knew in their head

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:16.095
probably an extra 60 to
90 days in the schedule.

00:07:16.095 --> 00:07:16.720
They committed.

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:19.990
But they figured by the time
things get through procurement,

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:22.580
backlog of servers making
it to the data center,

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:25.810
by the time they get it in,
they rack it, put it in, put up

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:28.150
the operating system,
get it networked, we're

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:30.880
looking at somewhere
between 90, 120 days.

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:32.590
I've even seen as
long as 180 days

00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:35.450
to get procured services
into the data center.

00:07:35.450 --> 00:07:38.145
And so those kind of processes,
as Brian was talking about,

00:07:38.145 --> 00:07:39.520
when you're standing
up something

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:41.830
to try to detect
it and resolve it,

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:44.030
that resource may have
already been built and gone.

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:46.070
And I know you certainly
experienced that at EA.

00:07:46.070 --> 00:07:46.903
BRIAN JOHNSON: Yeah.

00:07:46.903 --> 00:07:48.560
I mean, certainly
if we had 180 days,

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:52.120
games get built and thrown
away in 180 days, right?

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:55.170
So you have to be able
to move more quickly.

00:07:55.170 --> 00:07:57.280
And I think the other
thing we saw was not just

00:07:57.280 --> 00:07:58.650
the time it took to provision.

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:00.400
I mean, that certainly
took a lot of time.

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:03.437
And I'm never going to
rack another server again.

00:08:03.437 --> 00:08:05.230
But certainly the
other thing we saw

00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:07.360
was just when you were
going through this process

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:08.985
and working with
engineering and trying

00:08:08.985 --> 00:08:10.630
to talk about the
problems they're

00:08:10.630 --> 00:08:13.120
going to face when they
start to adopt cloud

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:15.250
and you're going to
that transformation,

00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:16.840
sometimes people
don't understand

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:19.220
the scale of the attack surface.

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:22.030
And so what I mean is
the offensive nature

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:23.480
of what's going on out there.

00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:25.035
So we used to have an exercise
we'd do with our engineers

00:08:25.035 --> 00:08:26.220
as they come on board.

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:29.080
We'd have them deploy a server
into a secured environment,

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:31.900
with port 22 open to the
world and completely publicly

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:32.539
accessible.

00:08:32.539 --> 00:08:34.919
And we just set like root,
root on password and log in.

00:08:34.919 --> 00:08:36.280
And we'd just have them time it.

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:38.679
How long does it take
before that box get popped?

00:08:38.679 --> 00:08:40.659
And sometimes when you
go through the exercise,

00:08:40.659 --> 00:08:42.520
it opens your eyes to
the amount of things

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:45.160
that are just out there
scanning and looking and trying

00:08:45.160 --> 00:08:46.470
to find ways in.

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:49.390
I mean, 10 or 12
years ago, there

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:51.820
was an increase in the amount
of sophisticated exploits

00:08:51.820 --> 00:08:53.110
that were being developed.

00:08:53.110 --> 00:08:54.920
That's actually started to
dovetail down a little bit,

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:56.795
primarily because it's
not necessary anymore.

00:08:56.795 --> 00:08:58.600
People are out there
opening up S3 buckets

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:00.830
or leaving databases
open to the world.

00:09:00.830 --> 00:09:02.050
I know that hasn't happened.

00:09:02.050 --> 00:09:02.800
THOMAS MARTIN: No.

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:03.330
BRIAN JOHNSON:
Like those things.

00:09:03.330 --> 00:09:05.540
And so it's not necessary
to spend time, energy,

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:08.040
and resources in those really
complex exploits, when you can

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:09.730
just scan and find a way in.

00:09:09.730 --> 00:09:11.970
And so that's part of
this equation is not only

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:14.620
understanding as security
professionals what

00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:17.830
scale looks like internally, but
also training the engineering

00:09:17.830 --> 00:09:20.260
organization about what's
important about security,

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:22.000
how they need to
deploy, how they

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:24.340
need to think about
people trying to get in,

00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:26.010
because you can help
teach them as they

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:27.010
go through this process.

00:09:27.010 --> 00:09:28.060
Everyone's in a race.

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:29.190
Like obviously it's
going to get better.

00:09:29.190 --> 00:09:31.150
You're going to get faster
and more innovative.

00:09:31.150 --> 00:09:33.608
THOMAS MARTIN: Well, just think
about your own SLAs, right?

00:09:33.608 --> 00:09:37.120
I mean, what would be an SLA
from a typical event in a data

00:09:37.120 --> 00:09:39.760
center to when you're
going to respond to it?

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:43.210
How much data could be
lost if you had that cloud

00:09:43.210 --> 00:09:45.190
storage open to the world?

00:09:45.190 --> 00:09:46.940
So those are the
things to think about.

00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:49.173
And so how do you
begin to really

00:09:49.173 --> 00:09:51.340
think about it from a
remediation standpoint, right?

00:09:51.340 --> 00:09:55.630
So the first off is it
needs to be near-real-time.

00:09:55.630 --> 00:09:57.400
And so as you go
around, it's really

00:09:57.400 --> 00:09:59.830
starting at a harvesting
point, so utilizing

00:09:59.830 --> 00:10:01.960
all the access
points, those APIs,

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:05.650
across all those resources, and
harvesting them back real-time,

00:10:05.650 --> 00:10:09.550
not only upon creation,
but actually upon change.

00:10:09.550 --> 00:10:11.215
That day 2 drift to
also think about.

00:10:11.215 --> 00:10:13.090
Things might've been
great as you deployed it

00:10:13.090 --> 00:10:14.270
out the CI/CD tool chain.

00:10:14.270 --> 00:10:17.560
But what happened after that
point with that engineer?

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:18.783
I honestly don't think--

00:10:18.783 --> 00:10:20.950
and one, I really don't
believe people intentionally

00:10:20.950 --> 00:10:23.320
do a lot of the configuration
mistakes they do.

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:24.820
But it's that
middle of the night,

00:10:24.820 --> 00:10:26.350
day 2 ops when
something's wrong,

00:10:26.350 --> 00:10:28.423
I'll change that back
as soon as it's resolved

00:10:28.423 --> 00:10:29.590
and it doesn't get resolved.

00:10:29.590 --> 00:10:31.360
It doesn't get
flipped back, right?

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:34.060
So you've first got to
harvest that data back in.

00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:36.760
Then you want to unify it
so that it's consistent

00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:40.330
across all of your individual
accounts, all of your VPCs.

00:10:40.330 --> 00:10:43.120
All those resources are then
normalized into a single data

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:44.410
plane.

00:10:44.410 --> 00:10:46.210
Then you want to drive
analysis against it.

00:10:46.210 --> 00:10:50.110
So as you thought about
establishing those compliance

00:10:50.110 --> 00:10:52.240
and security
policies of what does

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:55.130
it mean to our organization
to be compliant,

00:10:55.130 --> 00:10:57.280
that's the analysis
that gets done real-time

00:10:57.280 --> 00:10:58.702
against those resources.

00:10:58.702 --> 00:11:00.160
And then being able
to take action.

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:03.500
What do I want to have
happen when this occurs?

00:11:03.500 --> 00:11:05.840
So it's that if,
then, this scenario.

00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:08.418
If port 22 is open to the
world, what do I want to do?

00:11:08.418 --> 00:11:09.460
Who do I want to wake up?

00:11:09.460 --> 00:11:11.470
What immediate action
do I want to take,

00:11:11.470 --> 00:11:13.990
not only to protect
the company, but also

00:11:13.990 --> 00:11:16.930
from a forensics perspective,
as well as to learn.

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:18.910
Was it the team
that inadvertently

00:11:18.910 --> 00:11:20.260
did it to resolve an issue?

00:11:20.260 --> 00:11:21.770
Or were we actually breached?

00:11:21.770 --> 00:11:24.625
So all that data is captured
and dumped off for analytics.

00:11:24.625 --> 00:11:25.500
BRIAN JOHNSON: So I--

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:25.960
THOMAS MARTIN: Yeah.

00:11:25.960 --> 00:11:26.630
BRIAN JOHNSON: One
thing here and unify.

00:11:26.630 --> 00:11:26.860
THOMAS MARTIN: Sure.

00:11:26.860 --> 00:11:29.485
BRIAN JOHNSON: I mean, everyone
saw this morning's announcement

00:11:29.485 --> 00:11:31.770
about the multi-cloud
push from Google.

00:11:31.770 --> 00:11:34.063
This is absolutely
the right way to go.

00:11:34.063 --> 00:11:35.730
I'm super-excited
about their doing this

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:37.370
and they're doing on
top of Kubernetes.

00:11:37.370 --> 00:11:39.300
Kubernetes is going
to be the element that

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:42.113
breaks down the barriers and
commoditizes infrastructure.

00:11:42.113 --> 00:11:43.530
And so at the end
of the day, it's

00:11:43.530 --> 00:11:45.620
going to be really important
from the enterprise

00:11:45.620 --> 00:11:47.120
organization
perspective when you're

00:11:47.120 --> 00:11:48.578
looking at the
infrastructure layer

00:11:48.578 --> 00:11:50.907
that you can have a unified
model, because you're

00:11:50.907 --> 00:11:52.740
going to have engineers
who are using Azure.

00:11:52.740 --> 00:11:54.960
You're going to have
engineers who are using GCP.

00:11:54.960 --> 00:11:56.490
You're going to have engineers
who are using Amazon.

00:11:56.490 --> 00:11:58.215
And you can't
build policies that

00:11:58.215 --> 00:12:00.270
are going to be just
living in those worlds,

00:12:00.270 --> 00:12:01.500
because you're going
to forget about them.

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:03.480
Or they're going to
sort of die on the vine

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:04.620
or in different ways go on.

00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:06.630
And as we know in
security, it doesn't matter

00:12:06.630 --> 00:12:08.940
if you have 95% coverage.

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:11.230
That 5% is the one
that's going to get you.

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:12.688
So you need to make
sure you create

00:12:12.688 --> 00:12:17.500
a holistic strategy and holistic
policy as you move forward.

00:12:17.500 --> 00:12:19.110
So how do you do that?

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:20.610
Well, there's lots
of different ways

00:12:20.610 --> 00:12:24.120
to think about dealing with
this using remediation.

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:26.640
One is in a development
environment,

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:28.140
your remediation
might be slightly

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:29.640
different than as your
production environment.

00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:31.015
And your development
environment,

00:12:31.015 --> 00:12:33.990
you can say, look, engineer, you
want do some latency testing.

00:12:33.990 --> 00:12:35.002
I work for a big bank.

00:12:35.002 --> 00:12:37.210
We are not allowed to have
servers outside the United

00:12:37.210 --> 00:12:37.800
States.

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:39.370
But you want do some
latency testing.

00:12:39.370 --> 00:12:40.560
So in the development
environment,

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:42.540
you can spin up an instance
in Asia Pac for the next two

00:12:42.540 --> 00:12:43.200
hours.

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:44.340
Two hours later,
our system's going

00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:46.132
to automatically come
back and clean it up,

00:12:46.132 --> 00:12:47.370
make sure everything's OK.

00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:49.847
But when you move that same
application to the staging,

00:12:49.847 --> 00:12:51.930
you may not actually have
that ability to do that.

00:12:51.930 --> 00:12:55.320
You might leverage more
faster remediation.

00:12:55.320 --> 00:12:58.030
A server comes on in Asia
Pac, it's killed instantly.

00:12:58.030 --> 00:13:00.030
But you still want to let
them have that ability

00:13:00.030 --> 00:13:02.160
to try new services and
do more things, right?

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:04.987
You don't want to lock them
down using preventive controls,

00:13:04.987 --> 00:13:07.320
because you need them to go
in there and try new things.

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:08.403
You need them to innovate.

00:13:08.403 --> 00:13:10.320
And if you block them
at the top layer,

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:11.820
they're just going
to go around you.

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:14.200
They'll go create an account
and try it themselves.

00:13:14.200 --> 00:13:16.200
And that's the worst
thing you can be in.

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:17.750
Being compromised is bad.

00:13:17.750 --> 00:13:20.730
Being compromised and not
knowing it is way worse.

00:13:20.730 --> 00:13:22.350
So embrace this.

00:13:22.350 --> 00:13:23.550
Help them innovate.

00:13:23.550 --> 00:13:24.905
Help them learn.

00:13:24.905 --> 00:13:26.280
Go through that
process with them

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:28.715
and leverage
remediation in real-time

00:13:28.715 --> 00:13:30.090
to be able to
provide flexibility

00:13:30.090 --> 00:13:31.350
about how they do that.

00:13:31.350 --> 00:13:32.850
But then when you
get to production,

00:13:32.850 --> 00:13:34.433
this is where you
may want to leverage

00:13:34.433 --> 00:13:35.670
some preventative controls.

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:37.680
The cloud providers today
provide different ways

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:40.097
to do preventative controls
and lock down certain services

00:13:40.097 --> 00:13:41.080
from being used.

00:13:41.080 --> 00:13:44.220
So as you're taking your
engineer through this journey,

00:13:44.220 --> 00:13:46.135
you want them to
each stage understand

00:13:46.135 --> 00:13:47.510
it's a little bit
more stringent,

00:13:47.510 --> 00:13:49.200
it's a little bit tighter,
it's a little bit harder

00:13:49.200 --> 00:13:50.390
to do what you're going
to do if it doesn't fall

00:13:50.390 --> 00:13:52.740
inside the parameters
of what we've approved,

00:13:52.740 --> 00:13:54.948
so when they get in production,
it just doesn't work.

00:13:54.948 --> 00:13:56.491
But they're not
surprised by the time

00:13:56.491 --> 00:13:58.866
they get there, because the
whole way through the journey

00:13:58.866 --> 00:13:59.949
you've been teaching them.

00:13:59.949 --> 00:14:01.800
And what's more important
about that is it's

00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:03.450
not just about
enforcing a policy

00:14:03.450 --> 00:14:05.010
and then just running away.

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:06.300
It's about engaging them.

00:14:06.300 --> 00:14:07.680
It's about bringing them into
the conversation and saying,

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:09.400
what is it you're
trying to accomplish?

00:14:09.400 --> 00:14:10.140
What are you trying to do?

00:14:10.140 --> 00:14:12.360
Let me help you find a
secure way of doing that.

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:15.080
Help them innovate and help
them along that journey.

00:14:15.080 --> 00:14:15.780
THOMAS MARTIN: Let
me interject there.

00:14:15.780 --> 00:14:17.280
And Brian's going to talk
about it on the next slide.

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:19.447
But the thing about it too,
it's, as he's mentioned,

00:14:19.447 --> 00:14:21.220
this funnel of restriction.

00:14:21.220 --> 00:14:23.010
You're providing
guardrails that are

00:14:23.010 --> 00:14:26.820
much wider in that early
stage to generate innovation.

00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:28.520
But by the time you
are at stage three,

00:14:28.520 --> 00:14:30.330
it's least privileged, right?

00:14:30.330 --> 00:14:31.723
In fact, in many
cases, it's just

00:14:31.723 --> 00:14:33.390
going to be machine-only
privileges that

00:14:33.390 --> 00:14:37.143
are enabled in production to
be able to run those services.

00:14:37.143 --> 00:14:39.060
And so we'll talk later
here in the next layer

00:14:39.060 --> 00:14:40.687
about how you get there.

00:14:40.687 --> 00:14:41.520
BRIAN JOHNSON: Yeah.

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:43.355
So let's talk about
these different layers.

00:14:43.355 --> 00:14:44.730
It's sort of like
almost thinking

00:14:44.730 --> 00:14:47.370
about super-fine-grained up
to a more coarse grain, right?

00:14:47.370 --> 00:14:49.870
So when you're in what
we call protect mode,

00:14:49.870 --> 00:14:52.170
this is where you're leveraging
real-time remediation

00:14:52.170 --> 00:14:53.940
to go in and clean
up after things

00:14:53.940 --> 00:14:56.715
or shut things down or clean
up security groups, whatever

00:14:56.715 --> 00:14:59.490
it might be, identify databases
that have not been connected

00:14:59.490 --> 00:15:00.240
to in a long time.

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:01.400
All those different
elements are going in,

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:03.567
automating, cleaning up,
fixing, that kind of stuff.

00:15:03.567 --> 00:15:06.460
And you're protecting your
environment on a regular basis.

00:15:06.460 --> 00:15:08.220
Then you have your
in-flight checks.

00:15:08.220 --> 00:15:10.170
So this is your
ability to take things

00:15:10.170 --> 00:15:14.043
like Terraform or CloudFormation
templates or anything

00:15:14.043 --> 00:15:16.710
you need to work with to be able
to deploy into your environment

00:15:16.710 --> 00:15:18.780
and provision, maybe
Helm Chart, where you're

00:15:18.780 --> 00:15:20.550
able to deploy into
that infrastructure

00:15:20.550 --> 00:15:23.040
and have the engineers
integrate with a tool that

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:26.410
will allow it to check those
things as they're doing it.

00:15:26.410 --> 00:15:28.127
So when they go into
the CI/DC process,

00:15:28.127 --> 00:15:29.710
it checks in with
the system and goes,

00:15:29.710 --> 00:15:30.885
hey, I'm about to build
these 10 resources.

00:15:30.885 --> 00:15:32.190
This is what it looks like.

00:15:32.190 --> 00:15:33.840
Is this OK?

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:36.463
Have the CI/DC process then
either pass it and say,

00:15:36.463 --> 00:15:38.880
yeah, you're allowed to do
this, because it's development,

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:40.797
but we're going to tell
you this is a problem,

00:15:40.797 --> 00:15:42.680
or have it just
straight fail the build.

00:15:42.680 --> 00:15:46.080
You want to integrate and bring
security into their world, not

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:47.310
the other way around.

00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:48.935
Again, if you try
and do that, they're

00:15:48.935 --> 00:15:50.610
just going to find it
annoying and go around you.

00:15:50.610 --> 00:15:52.652
So how do you go-- how do
you bring it in, right?

00:15:52.652 --> 00:15:55.120
So those in-flight checks
are really important.

00:15:55.120 --> 00:15:57.090
And then finally, these
sort of landing spaces,

00:15:57.090 --> 00:15:59.120
this idea that as you
provision accounts,

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:00.900
you do it in an
automated fashion.

00:16:00.900 --> 00:16:03.420
And when you do that
for projects or teams

00:16:03.420 --> 00:16:06.390
or whatever it might be, you go
in and start slapping controls

00:16:06.390 --> 00:16:07.850
around it at provision time.

00:16:07.850 --> 00:16:09.960
And this might be a
mixture of remediation

00:16:09.960 --> 00:16:11.700
and preventative measures.

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:14.827
You might enforce some sort of
ability with CI/CD pipeline,

00:16:14.827 --> 00:16:16.410
where it's getting
checked as it goes.

00:16:16.410 --> 00:16:18.330
All those sort of tighten
things around as you

00:16:18.330 --> 00:16:21.910
go into more production
and preventative accounts.

00:16:21.910 --> 00:16:22.858
There we go.

00:16:22.858 --> 00:16:24.400
THOMAS MARTIN: And
so those really go

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:25.410
in combination, right?

00:16:25.410 --> 00:16:28.380
So those coarse-grained
or those big mindsets

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:31.950
that say, these are
never to be violated,

00:16:31.950 --> 00:16:34.290
if you will, down
to that mid-grain,

00:16:34.290 --> 00:16:37.278
where, as Brian talked about,
you may put a warning there

00:16:37.278 --> 00:16:39.570
in that dev cycle, but you're
not going to shut it down

00:16:39.570 --> 00:16:41.580
immediately, because
you also are facing

00:16:41.580 --> 00:16:43.560
into that cultural
shift, so you also

00:16:43.560 --> 00:16:46.200
want to educate engineering
as to why we are going

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:49.290
that direction, down to those
fine-grain controls that

00:16:49.290 --> 00:16:51.390
not only take care
of upon launch,

00:16:51.390 --> 00:16:55.737
but really that drift that
can occur day 2, day 30.

00:16:55.737 --> 00:16:57.570
So those really combined
with some of things

00:16:57.570 --> 00:16:59.945
that we talk about on the
previous slide around the cycle

00:16:59.945 --> 00:17:01.200
aspect gives us--

00:17:01.200 --> 00:17:03.420
for those leaders in the
room, gives us that ability

00:17:03.420 --> 00:17:05.490
to become more the
department of yes,

00:17:05.490 --> 00:17:07.263
to drive innovation
for your company,

00:17:07.263 --> 00:17:08.430
versus the department of no.

00:17:11.329 --> 00:17:14.069
BRIAN JOHNSON: Look, I think
the high-level key takeaway here

00:17:14.069 --> 00:17:16.589
is that as everyone
goes down this journey,

00:17:16.589 --> 00:17:20.670
you need to define a strategy
for the organization.

00:17:20.670 --> 00:17:21.295
This is not a--

00:17:21.295 --> 00:17:23.670
this is why we started out
talking about the fact this is

00:17:23.670 --> 00:17:25.060
not just a technology problem.

00:17:25.060 --> 00:17:27.390
This is how a business
is going to transform.

00:17:27.390 --> 00:17:29.448
When I was at EA and
we were building games,

00:17:29.448 --> 00:17:30.990
the introduction of
cloud didn't just

00:17:30.990 --> 00:17:32.880
change how we deployed
our applications.

00:17:32.880 --> 00:17:35.580
It literally changed what
applications we built.

00:17:35.580 --> 00:17:38.010
It changed what games
we took to market.

00:17:38.010 --> 00:17:41.130
It changed which games we
decided to stop developing on,

00:17:41.130 --> 00:17:43.200
because we were able
to do it faster.

00:17:43.200 --> 00:17:44.955
So it's a huge business
transformation.

00:17:44.955 --> 00:17:46.770
So as you go through
this process,

00:17:46.770 --> 00:17:49.620
you decide how
security, how IT, how

00:17:49.620 --> 00:17:51.390
cloud ops is going
to address this,

00:17:51.390 --> 00:17:54.120
it's important to think of
about it as a holistic strategy.

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:56.670
So when we talk
about those layers,

00:17:56.670 --> 00:17:58.560
all the way from
development into production

00:17:58.560 --> 00:18:00.240
needs to be taken
into consideration,

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:02.400
and how you engage
your engineering staff

00:18:02.400 --> 00:18:04.490
and teach them as they go.

00:18:04.490 --> 00:18:05.950
THOMAS MARTIN: Absolutely.

00:18:05.950 --> 00:18:09.000
And I think, as Brian mentioned
earlier around filtering out

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:12.540
the noise, we can't rely on the
traditional just the perimeter

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:15.880
security control and just
providing notification.

00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:16.380
OK.

00:18:16.380 --> 00:18:19.020
It's great to know that there's
a theft happening in aisle 5.

00:18:19.020 --> 00:18:22.230
But have we filtered out the
noise and know exactly where

00:18:22.230 --> 00:18:25.140
and pinpoint what is happening,
where it's happening,

00:18:25.140 --> 00:18:26.850
and how to resolve
it, and then be

00:18:26.850 --> 00:18:28.980
able to take that
action to remediate it

00:18:28.980 --> 00:18:31.292
in a time of cloud speed?

00:18:31.292 --> 00:18:33.000
BRIAN JOHNSON: The
other thing-- so we're

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:35.280
out there on a regular basis
working with large enterprise

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:37.270
customers, solving for
this strategic problem.

00:18:37.270 --> 00:18:38.370
And one of the things
that we've found

00:18:38.370 --> 00:18:40.412
is that companies that
tend to have most success,

00:18:40.412 --> 00:18:43.290
are able to get moving quickest,
have an established cloud ops

00:18:43.290 --> 00:18:43.922
team.

00:18:43.922 --> 00:18:45.630
This is sort of
interesting, because when

00:18:45.630 --> 00:18:47.970
we talk about security,
there's this desire

00:18:47.970 --> 00:18:50.700
to think about traditional
infrastructure security.

00:18:50.700 --> 00:18:53.160
This is about analyzing
network traffic

00:18:53.160 --> 00:18:55.740
and identifying external
threats and coming up

00:18:55.740 --> 00:18:59.032
with preventative measures
to react to those threats.

00:18:59.032 --> 00:19:00.990
But the security problem
we're facing right now

00:19:00.990 --> 00:19:03.120
is different than what
we've seen before.

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:05.670
I used to do exploit development
professionally for a while.

00:19:05.670 --> 00:19:07.140
And so from the
offensive side, you're

00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:08.370
thinking about things
slightly different.

00:19:08.370 --> 00:19:10.470
You're thinking about how
to get into a black box.

00:19:10.470 --> 00:19:12.870
From the security side, when
you're defending against that,

00:19:12.870 --> 00:19:14.400
you're looking at traffic
to try and figure out

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:15.570
what people are
throwing at you, what

00:19:15.570 --> 00:19:16.990
do they know about you
that you don't know,

00:19:16.990 --> 00:19:18.240
and so on and so forth.

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:20.460
When you're dealing with
the cloud ops side of things

00:19:20.460 --> 00:19:22.260
and helping
engineering teams grow,

00:19:22.260 --> 00:19:23.765
it's much more
about understanding

00:19:23.765 --> 00:19:25.890
what they're doing and what
their needs are, making

00:19:25.890 --> 00:19:27.140
sure they don't make mistakes.

00:19:27.140 --> 00:19:28.220
It's an internal threat.

00:19:28.220 --> 00:19:30.420
And it's very different,
because it turns out you

00:19:30.420 --> 00:19:32.150
and them are on the same side.

00:19:32.150 --> 00:19:33.980
You're not fighting one another.

00:19:33.980 --> 00:19:36.142
So you have to find a
way to embrace that.

00:19:36.142 --> 00:19:38.100
And what we've found is
by establishing a cloud

00:19:38.100 --> 00:19:40.230
center of excellence,
a cloud ops team that's

00:19:40.230 --> 00:19:42.960
going to be focused on security
from a cloud perspective

00:19:42.960 --> 00:19:45.120
and what that means to
the internal organization

00:19:45.120 --> 00:19:48.280
means you get a lot more
innovation a lot quicker.

00:19:48.280 --> 00:19:51.150
THOMAS MARTIN: I think to add to
that is my experience has also

00:19:51.150 --> 00:19:52.950
been that having
that focal team,

00:19:52.950 --> 00:19:55.350
that cloud ops team also
helps as an accelerant,

00:19:55.350 --> 00:19:57.030
not only from an
adoption perspective,

00:19:57.030 --> 00:19:59.880
but also from an educational,
cultural perspective

00:19:59.880 --> 00:20:02.730
across the entire
organization as folks

00:20:02.730 --> 00:20:05.342
begin to transition out of
that data center mindset.

00:20:05.342 --> 00:20:06.800
In many cases,
you're going to be--

00:20:06.800 --> 00:20:08.460
I mean, my expense
has been, again,

00:20:08.460 --> 00:20:10.030
that most organizations
of that size

00:20:10.030 --> 00:20:11.280
are going to always be hybrid.

00:20:11.280 --> 00:20:14.190
They're going to have their
data center with their large ERP

00:20:14.190 --> 00:20:18.070
systems and others that
will remain on prem.

00:20:18.070 --> 00:20:20.460
But to be able to manage that
mindset across the board,

00:20:20.460 --> 00:20:22.590
it helps to have
that cloud ops team.

00:20:22.590 --> 00:20:23.673
BRIAN JOHNSON: Absolutely.

00:20:23.673 --> 00:20:25.940
[MUSIC PLAYING]

